# WASM benchmark fixture: 2-BILLION-iteration FNV-style hash.
#
# Duo replacement for bench/hash2b.c. Same kernel as bench/hash.id with ten
# times the loop — it exists to give the harnesses a long-running module.
#
#   duo compile bench/hash2b.id #target wasm32-wasi -o bench/hash2b.wasm
#
# Returns i32, matching the C original's uint32_t -> wasm i32 signature, so
# wasmtime prints -1009008024 exactly as it did for the C build. Keeping the
# signed i32 preserves the one corpus entry that exercises verify.id's
# unsigned fold # the case that caught a real bug in it.
#
# Locals are `u32` so the TYPE carries the wrap, as C's uint32_t did. An
# earlier version used i64 plus `& 4294967295` after every step: same answer,
# three extra ops per iteration, which pushed the engine's INTERPRETER past the
# harnesses' 120s timeout on the 2-billion sibling. A fixture must be fast
# enough to time.

# GAP-12: `@c.export` does not attach to a value-form binding — it degrades
# to "use of undeclared identifier '__c_export'". The exported entry stays a
# bare declaration until that closes; `main` uses the canonical value form.
@comp.c.export("run")
run(): i32
    h: u32 = 2166136261
    i: u32 = 0
    while i < 2000000000
        h = h ~ i
        h = h * 16777619
        h = (h << 13) | (h >> 19)
        i += 1
    end
    h
end

main: i32 = ()
    run()
end
